\DOC LOCAL_SQR_P521_TAC

\TYPE {LOCAL_SQR_P521_TAC : int -> string list -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Advances an {eventually}-style machine-state goal across one inlined
modular-squaring subroutine call, replacing the current symbolic state by a
later one and recording the squaring result as a new assumption.

\DESCRIBE
The first argument is an {int} offset/count controlling how many extra
instructions (e.g. argument setup before the call body) are consumed before the
subroutine proper; the second is a {string list} naming the output and input
operands of the squaring step, the first name becoming the result variable and
the second identifying the source operand. Applied to a goal whose conclusion
is {eventually ... s} for a current state variable, it produces a single
subgoal whose conclusion is the same {eventually} property at a fresh, later
state variable. The assumptions are reorganised: the old state's
program-counter, register, memory-content and {bytes_loaded} assumptions (and
any initial trivial {MAYCHANGE} reflexivity assumption) are discharged and
re-established at the new state, with an updated program counter and an
enlarged accumulated {MAYCHANGE} relation between the initial and the new
state; in addition a new memory-content assumption records the result value at
its destination, and a new conditional arithmetic assumption relating the named
input and output is added.
{
         A ?- eventually ... s
   =========================================  LOCAL_SQR_P521_TAC n [u; t]
         A' ?- eventually ... s'
}
\noindent where {A'} is {A} with the state-dependent assumptions restated at
{s'}, plus the new result assumption and the squaring-correctness assumption {t
< p_521 ==> u = t EXP 2 MOD p_521}.

\COMMENTS
In all observed traces only this single kind of transformation occurs, always
on goals of this shape with a symbolic machine state.

\ENDDOC
